<p>British Prime Minister Keir Starmer told Parliament on April 20 that he made the wrong call in appointing Peter Mandelson as the U.K. ambassador to Washington. He said he would not have done so had he known Mandelson had failed a security vetting process. Starmer said neither he nor other ministers learned until the previous week that officials had advised against granting Mandelson the highest level of clearance, turning the issue into a fresh controversy over accountability inside government. Starmer blamed Foreign Office officials for withholding the information and said they had deliberately chosen not to share it with senior ministers. Critics seized on the episode to question his judgment and renew calls for his resignation. The affair has also sharpened scrutiny of how the government handles sensitive diplomatic appointments, especially for the U.K.&#x27;s key post in Washington, and opened a wider dispute over whether civil servants were legally required to keep the vetting recommendation confidential.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/OT4R00T_De8hMWq8sxuWfEyEs1YEgXg24ncqbilgBU1-p5NgHu0EIUfn08Whf1lO5I_u0fXyCCC85vOnxTZWrJClE9mKHUqcGHhr14HJI3999bzUNyY3PShvaPF0n5E0tF1p0o6CKxbHilU3dlDFmeVu88sBty5TA9xI3bRIpZwDKf9fipqrescQSCQgQSnhMDyVZoTdVurkgDRwHwCgklq5MUWU3MVIjFs6xjo0xGD1S_SBdFMrhNgQW6Lnk6dIoTCRQ82WYNuYgycatA' alt='Britain&#x27;s Prime Minister Keir Starmer leaves 10 Downing Street in London on April 20, 2026, before facing Parliament over the Mandelson appointment.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Security level: The disputed clearance was &quot;developed vetting,&quot; which allows access to information classified as top secret, according to Starmer&#x27;s account in Parliament.</li><li>Committee focus: Oliver Robbins was due to give evidence to a committee of MPs on Tuesday, in a hearing that could clarify who knew what and when.</li><li>Political stakes: Several reports said opposition figures and other critics stepped up calls for Starmer to resign as the scandal dominated Parliament and British politics.</li></ul><blockquote>It beggars belief that throughout the whole timeline of events, officials in the Foreign Office saw fit to withhold this information from the most senior ministers in our system, in the government. - Keir Starmer</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Keir Starmer: Starmer said he would never have appointed Mandelson had he known about the failed vetting recommendation, and argued that officials kept ministers in the dark. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/uk-starmer-says-he-would-t-have-appointed-mandelson-as-us-envoy-if-he-d-known-he-failed-vetting-process/a-76864691'>DW</a>)</li><li>Foreign Office officials: Officials are at the center of the dispute because Starmer says they withheld the recommendation, while Oliver Robbins was due to face MPs&#x27; questions about who knew what and when. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/7-reasons-starmer-cant-shake-off-the-mandelson-vetting-saga/'>POLITICO Europe</a>)</li><li>Political opponents: Opponents have treated the affair as evidence of poor judgment at the top of government and pressed Starmer to resign over the appointment. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3350783/starmer-admits-mistake-appointing-mandelson-us-envoy-resists-calls-resign'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Analysts and media: Coverage in several outlets suggests the row now goes beyond a single appointment to broader questions about Starmer&#x27;s control over his office, his relationship with civil servants, and the public&#x27;s tolerance for renewed scandal. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/7-reasons-starmer-cant-shake-off-the-mandelson-vetting-saga/'>POLITICO Europe</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/starmer-says-he-made-wrong-judgment-in-appointing-peter-mandelson-as-ambassador'>Starmer says he made wrong judgment in appointing Peter Mandelson as ambassador</a> - pbs.org</l